nodecode-git-hook is a rewrite of aean0x's aean0x/hermes-git-hook:

  https://github.com/aean0x/hermes-git-hook
  commit a7303c89b429704a31e2df4212ec4f08ee76692a
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 aean0x (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the four callbacks as two hook points. Hermes Agent calls
pre_tool_call, post_tool_call, post_llm_call and on_session_end; Nodecode has
one point around a tool call (:TOOL, wrapping the call, so the before and the
after are the two sides of one function) and one before a model request
(:ROUND), which is where a turn's end lands and the queue is flushed. The
contract is kept whole: the allowlist of roots, the dirty-gated
ff-only pull once per worktree per session, the value-carrying dirty set (a
path's status, size and write date, so an edit to a file that was already
dirty is still a delta, and dirt nobody touched is left alone), staging only
the turn's own paths with literal pathspecs, the secret and scratch-name
filters, `-c core.fsmonitor=` and no terminal prompts on every run, a commit
with `user.useConfigOnly`, push to the branch's own upstream, the policy
refusal that drops the hook's own commit with `reset --soft` and closes the
branch for the session, and fail-open at every step so a hook can never fail
the tool call it wraps. Git runs through the kernel's bounded subprocess seam
instead of `subprocess.run`, so no polling loop was needed. The notes the
original returned from its hooks (which Hermes discarded) go to the board as
one standing notice, where the operator sees them and the model reads them in
the next request.

Left out: the environment-variable knobs (GIT_HOOK_ROOTS, GIT_HOOK_COMMIT,
GIT_HOOK_PUSH, GIT_HOOK_COMMIT_MSG, GIT_HOOK_COMMIT_PATH, the timeout and
budget variables, PROJECTS_ROOT, HERMES_HOME) -- the `git-hook` section
carries them as typed settings, and `/git-hook` moves the two switches at
run time. The `fcntl` lock file inside the worktree's git directory is gone:
the cell serializes its own state with one in-image lock and lets git's own
index lock do the rest, so nothing of its own is written into a repository.
The stale-path rescue (`git ls-files` for paths a turn created and deleted) is
kept, as is the per-worktree upstream check. Left out too: the `mtime_ns`
precision of the signature (file-write-date is second-granular, as Nodecode's
own staleness memos are), the "unpushed retry" bookkeeping of a commit that
failed on a *transient* push error is kept in memory only, and /tmp and
/var/tmp are no longer excluded from the sync (a session's own scratch
worktree often lives there), while /nix, /proc, /sys, /dev and /run still are.
